A data protection impact assessment is required prior to processing where a type of processing, in particular using new technologies, is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons, taking into account the nature, scope, context, and purposes of the processing [1]. It is in particular required for:  
- a systematic and extensive evaluation of personal aspects based on automated processing, including profiling, where decisions produce legal effects or similarly significantly affect the person [1];  
- processing on a large scale of special categories of data under Article 9(1), or personal data relating to criminal convictions and offences under Article 10 [1];  
- systematic monitoring of a publicly accessible area on a large scale [1][3].

The assessment may cover a set of similar processing operations that present similar high risks [1].

The documents also say that certain processing under Article 6(1)(c) or (e) may be exempt from paragraphs 1 to 7 if a legal basis in Union or Member State law already includes a general impact assessment, unless Member States require an assessment before processing [2]. They do not provide a complete list of every other situation where a DPIA is or is not required beyond these cases.

A risk management system for high-risk AI systems must be established, implemented, documented, and maintained [3]. It has to be a continuous iterative process running throughout the entire lifecycle of the system, with regular systematic review and updating [3].

It must include:
- identification and analysis of known and reasonably foreseeable risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights when used as intended [3];
- estimation and evaluation of risks arising from intended use and reasonably foreseeable misuse [3];
- evaluation of other risks based on post-market monitoring data [3];
- adoption of appropriate and targeted measures to address the identified risks [3].

The risks covered are only those that can reasonably be mitigated or eliminated through the system’s development or design, or through adequate technical information [3].

The risk management process must also consider whether the system may adversely affect persons under 18 and, where appropriate, other vulnerable groups [5]. Testing must be used to identify suitable risk management measures, ensure consistent performance for the intended purpose, and verify compliance with the relevant requirements; it may include real-world testing, and must be done throughout development and before market placement or putting into service [2]. Providers must also consider the deployer’s technical knowledge, experience, education, expected training, and intended context of use when reducing risks [2].

The documents also say that for providers subject to other Union-law internal risk management requirements, these aspects may be part of or combined with those procedures [5].

The documents do not say everything about practical internal governance beyond these requirements, but they do specify that the quality management system must include the risk management system [4].

Deployers of high-risk AI systems must:

- take appropriate technical and organisational measures to use the system in accordance with the instructions for use [1];
- assign human oversight to natural persons who have the necessary competence, training, authority, and support [1];
- ensure, where they control the input data, that the input data is relevant and sufficiently representative for the intended purpose [1][3];
- monitor the operation of the high-risk AI system on the basis of the instructions for use and, where relevant, inform providers in accordance with Article 72 [3];
- if they have reason to think use in accordance with the instructions may present a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1), they must without undue delay inform the provider or distributor and the relevant market surveillance authority, and suspend use of the system [3];
- if they identify a serious incident, they must immediately inform first the provider, then the importer or distributor and the relevant market surveillance authorities [3];
- if they are financial institutions subject to Union financial services law requirements on internal governance, arrangements or processes, they must maintain logs as part of the documentation kept under that law [4];
- before putting a high-risk AI system into service or using it at the workplace, employer-deployers must inform workers’ representatives and affected workers [4];
- public authorities and Union institutions, bodies, offices or agencies must comply with the registration obligations in Article 49, and must not use a system that has not been registered in the EU database; they must inform the provider or distributor if it has not been registered [4];
- where applicable, they must use the information under Article 13 to comply with their data protection impact assessment obligations under GDPR Article 35 or Directive (EU) 2016/680 Article 27 [4].

On impact assessments, some deployers must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment before deploying the high-risk AI system: this applies to deployers that are bodies governed by public law, private entities providing public services, and deployers of certain high-risk AI systems listed in Annex III points 5(b) and 5(c), with the exception of high-risk AI systems intended to be used in the area listed in point 2 of Annex III [2]. That assessment must include the items listed in Article 27(1)(a) to (f), including the deployer’s processes, intended period and frequency of use, affected persons, risks of harm, human oversight measures, and measures for when risks materialise [2]. The assessment applies to the first use, may rely on previous assessments in similar cases, and must be updated if relevant elements change or are no longer up to date [5]. After performing it, the deployer must notify the market surveillance authority of the results, using the filled-out template, unless an exemption in Article 46(1) applies [5]. If these obligations are already met through a data protection impact assessment, the fundamental rights impact assessment must complement that assessment [5].

The documents do not cover general deployer obligations outside the quoted provisions, nor do they explain all details of Article 49 registration or Article 72 reporting procedures [4][3].

Under DORA, contractual arrangements for ICT services must be in writing, with the rights and obligations of both parties clearly allocated, and the full contract including service level agreements in one written document available in a durable, accessible format [1].

At a minimum, the contract must include:
- a clear and complete description of all functions and ICT services, including whether subcontracting of critical/important services or material parts is allowed and on what conditions [1];
- the locations where functions/services and data processing (including storage) will occur, plus advance notice of any location changes [1];
- provisions on availability, authenticity, integrity and confidentiality of data, including personal data [1];
- access, recovery and return of personal and non-personal data in an easily accessible format if the provider becomes insolvent, is resolved, discontinues business, or the contract ends [2];
- service level descriptions, including updates and revisions [2];
- assistance by the provider at no additional cost, or at a pre-agreed cost, when an ICT incident related to the service occurs [2];
- cooperation with the financial entity’s competent and resolution authorities, including their appointed persons [2];
- termination rights and minimum notice periods, in line with authority expectations [2];
- conditions for the provider’s participation in ICT security awareness programmes and digital operational resilience training [2][6].

For ICT services supporting critical or important functions, the contract must also include, in addition to the above:
- full service level descriptions with precise quantitative and qualitative performance targets to enable monitoring and timely corrective action [3];
- notice periods and reporting obligations, including notification of developments that could materially affect the provider’s ability to deliver the service at agreed levels [3];
- requirements to implement and test business contingency plans and maintain appropriate ICT security measures, tools and policies [3];
- details on the scope, procedures, and frequency of inspections and audits [4];
- exit strategies, including an adequate transition period to continue the service during transition and allow migration to another provider or in-house solutions [4].

The documents also say financial entities should consider standard contractual clauses developed by public authorities for specific services when negotiating these arrangements [4].

The documents do not cover every possible clause that might be included beyond these listed minimum requirements, nor do they explain any sector-specific drafting practice outside these DORA provisions [1][2][3][4].

The provided documents show several data subject rights under the GDPR, but they do not give a complete list of all rights “in general.”

Supported rights include:
- the right to obtain access to personal data and related information from the controller [5];
- the right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority [5];
- the right to request rectification, erasure, or restriction of processing, and to object to processing [5];
- rights related to automated decision-making, including profiling, with meaningful information about the logic and consequences of such processing [5], [7];
- the right to mandate a not-for-profit body, organisation, or association to lodge complaints and exercise rights under Articles 77, 78, 79, and 82 on the data subject’s behalf, where Member State law allows for compensation claims [2];
- rights in relation to binding corporate rules, including the right to lodge a complaint, seek redress, and where appropriate compensation for a breach of the rules [3].

The documents do not cover the full general catalogue of GDPR data subject rights beyond these examples, and they also do not provide a single consolidated overview of all rights.
